          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A theory of
            %em quantum gravity
            (QG) is supposed to be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            (QFT) – or something similar – that corresponds to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical field theory
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
          %p
            The key technical problem with quantum gravity as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} non-perturbative quantum field theory
            is that all known constructions of QFTs quantize
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            defined
            %em on
            a given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+field"} background
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            %em is
            that spacetime.
          %p
            Since even for field theories that are defined on space-time backgrounds, such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} non-perturbative
            quantization is a big open problem away from toy examples (for Yang-Mills theory it has been dubbed one of the
            = succeed "," do
              %em Millennium Problems
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization+of+Yang-Mills+theory"} quantization of Yang-Mills theory
          %p
            However, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Hilbert+action"} Einstein-Hilbert action
            that defines gravity may be expanded around any of its solutions to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein+equations"} Einstein equations
            and the resulting
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation"} perturbation
            of the field of gravity around this background behaves, to low order, like an ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field
            on a fixed spacetime background. This allows to treat
            %em perturbative quantum gravity
            (pQG) as a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#ReferencesAsAnEffectiveFieldTheory"} below
          %p
            While the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Hilbert+action"} Einstein-Hilbert action
            (or any of its variants) that defines the dynamics of gravity makes perfect sense at low energies as the definition of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+quantum+field+theory"} effective quantum field theory
            (see there for details), it is not expected to be a non-effective theory at arbitrary high energies, because it is
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalizable+interaction"} not renormalizable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-renormalizable+QFTs"} non-renormalizable QFTs
            that have appeared in practice turned out to be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+QFT"} effective QFT
            approximations (see there) to renormalizable
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UV-completions"} UV-completions
          %p
            The idea has been explored that gravity is simply to be regarded as a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stress-energy+tensor"} stress-energy tensor
            of a matter
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            leads to conceptual problems that break the very foundation of what is known about theoretical physics. The technical problem is this: the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            of classical Einstein
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein%27s+equations"}> Einstein's equations
            ) assert that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein+tensor"} Einstein tensor
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field (physics)
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            equals the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy-momentum+tensor"} energy-momentum tensor
            of all other
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/force"} force
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matter"} matter
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy-momentum+tensor"} energy-momentum tensor
            enters the expression for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy"} energy
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/momentum"} momentum
            of classical matter fields. However, according to the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein%27s+equations"} Einstein's equations
            so as to have a quantum EM tensor in it. The simple idea of taking the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/expectation+value"} expectation value
            of the quantum matter EM-tensor leads to all kinds of theoretical problems.
          %p
            Thus, this idea seems to be very unlikely. At the same time, it is clear that present experiments cannot
            %em directly
            probe the effects of that supposed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UV-completion"} UV-completion
            of Einstein-gravity.
          %p Approaches to a full quantization of gravity therefore roughly fall into two different strategies
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                One assumes that the Einstein-Hilbert action is indeed the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+QFT"} effective QFT
                that approximates a “
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UV-completion"}> UV-completion
                ”, a more fundamental theory valid at all energies. This is the approach taken for instance in
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            %li
              %p
                One assumes that by some other fact that has been overlooked, one can make sense of a non-perturbative quantization of the EH action at arbitrary energies after all. This is for instance the case in speculations that EH-gravity has a
                = succeed "." do
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    UV fixed point
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/UV+fixed+point"} ?
          %p
            Accordingly, there are various proposals for what quantum gravity might be, but a comprehensive theory is missing to date. This is in stark contrast to the situation for the other fundamental
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/force"} force
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+field"} Yang-Mills field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            is the main reason for the belief that QFT is the right framework for fundamental physics, and that also the field of gravity must have a description in this form.
          %p
            Indeed, a model of fundamental physics where Yang-Mills fields are quantized but gravity is not is incomplete and arguably even inconsistent. Central motivating examples for this come from
            = succeed "-physics:" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+hole"} black hole
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                The study of quantum fields on the classical gravitational background of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+hole"} black hole
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
                suggest that in a full theory of quantum gravity black holes could be shown to emit radiation and to eventually even decay in some way or other. This expected decay process cannot be described without a theory of quantum gravity.
            %li
              %p
                Related to black hole radiation is a whole series of observations in classical
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+relativity"} general relativity
                that seem to indicate that black holes need to be thought of as systems possessing physical microstates with an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/entropy"} entropy
                that cannot be accounted for by classical general relativity. This has led to the expectation that these microstates are states in quantum theory of gravity and that their understanding is necessary for understanding the physics of black holes.
            %li
              %p
                More generally, appearances of unphysical singularities in
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+relativity"} general relativity
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+hole"} black hole
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmology"} cosmological
                singularities such as the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/big+bang"} big bang
          %p
            An evident definition of quantum gravity would seem to simply be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Hilbert+action"} Einstein-Hilbert action
            by the rules
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            is not
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalizable
          %p
            More recently it has been argued that there is evidence that the EH-action has a nontrivial
            %span.newWikiWord
              UV-fixed point
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/UV-fixed+point"} ?
            after all. If true this would mean that a quantization of gravity in standard QFT is possible.
          %p
            Another possibility is that this is only an apparent problem, and that standard gravity is nothing but an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+quantum+field+theory"} effective quantum field theory
          %p But most approaches to the problem have taken the non-renormalizability of classical gravity to be an indication that the standard concepts of QFT might need refinement in order to include gravity.
          %p
            A common idea is that the assumption that the space of configurations in gravity is really that of smooth
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+geometry"} noncommutative geometry
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alain+Connes"} Alain Connes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+action"} spectral action
            generalizes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Hilbert+action"} Einstein-Hilbert action
            functional from
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifold"} Riemannian manifold
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+triple"} spectral triple
          %p
            Accordingly there are suggestions to modify instead the principles of perturbative quantum field theory. In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            the fundamental assumption is that the Feynman perturbation series over 1-dimensional graphs of traditional perturbative QFT is to be refined by a similar series over 2-dimensional surfaces. It has been shown that this
            %em string perturbation series
            needs no renormalization and that it does describe Yang-Mills fields and a gravitational field. This led to huge hopes and large activity in the study of string theory as a theory of quantum gravity. But various central questions remain open and the state of the theory remains somewhat inconclusive.
          %p
            While perturbative string theory did demonstrate that there is a variant of the perturbation series for gravity which is renormalized, it can, by definition of perturbation theory, be just an approximation to a more fundamental non-perturbatively defined theory. Various candidates for the non-perturbative theory to which the string perturbation series might be an approximation have been proposed. One that enjoyed a large popularity in the 1990s was called
            %em matrix mechanics
            but the high hopes connected with it were supported mostly by toy example computations. More recently the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS%2FCFT+correspondence"} AdS/CFT correspondence
            is widely thought of as providing a non-perturbative definition of string theory and of quantum gravity. This correspondence asserts that observables of a string theory on an asymptotically
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anti-de+Sitter+spacetime"} anti-de Sitter spacetime
            may be matched equivalently to certain observables in an auxiliary super
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
          %p
            Still another suggestion has been that ordinary quantum field theory might be seen to apply to the ordinary configuration space and action functional of general relativity, if only a suitably adapted parameterization of the configuration space is chosen. Notably one observes that
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifold"} Riemannian manifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connections on the tangent bundle
            – the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Levi-Civita+connection"} Levi-Civita connection
            – this being manifestly analogous to the nature of the fields in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+formulation+of+gravity"} first-order formulation of gravity
          %p
            It has been suggested that therefore quantum gravity may have a description in terms of the
            = succeed "-observables" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holonomy"} holonomy
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parallel+transport"} parallel transport
            around loops. Research in this direction has therefore become known as
            %em loop quantum gravity
            \. However, little progress has been made in understanding the configuration space of smooth connections in terms of loop observables. In most of the LQG-literature instead it is assumed from the outset that it makes sense to pass to
            %em generalized connections
            \: while a smooth connection is the same as a smooth
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parallel+transport"} parallel transport
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+groupoid"} path groupoid
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi P
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{P}_1(X) \to \mathbf{B}G
          %p
            A plethora of further suggestions has developed out of this, which in the literature often still go under the name “LQG”, even though they may abandon the description of configuration spaces of metrics in terms of loop observables. There is for instance the approach of
            %em spin foams
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+dynamical+triangulations"} causal dynamical triangulations
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causets"} causets
            , to name just a few.
          %p The problem with discriminating between all these proposals is the combination of two problems.
          %ol
            %li
              %p There exist insufficient theoretical tools for analyzing in detail the consequences of the various proposals.
            %li
              %p There are few to no experiments available that would be able to observe the phenomena that a theory of quantum gravity is supposed to describe.
          %p As a result, the field of fundamental physics today finds itself in a somewhat awkward position where the need for theoretical progress concerning quantum gravity for conceptual reasons is widely appreciated, but where disagreements about the viability of various proposals finds no conclusive resolution and divides the community.
          %h2#effects Effects
          %h3#FirstQuantumCorrectionToNewtonianPotential
            First quantum correction to Newtonian
            = succeed "-potential" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi r
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1/r
          %p The first order quantum correction to the classical gravitational potential
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi cl
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi r
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo −
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                      %msub
                        %mi m
                        %mn 1
                      %msub
                        %mi m
                        %mn 2
                    %mi r
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  V_{cl}(r) \;=\; - \frac{G m_1 m_2}{r}
          %p
            between two bodies of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass"} mass
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi m
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m_1
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi m
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m_2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative
            quantum gravity (
            %a{:href => "#Donoghue95"}> Donoghue 95, Section 9
            ):
          %p The result is a term of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi qu
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi r
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi cl
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi r
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo −
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mn 135
                        %mo +
                        %mn 2
                        %msub
                          %mi N
                          %mi ν
                      %mrow
                        %mn 30
                        %msup
                          %mi π
                          %mn 2
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                        %mi ℏ
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi r
                          %mn 2
                        %msup
                          %mi c
                          %mn 2
                    %mo +
                    %mtext higher order
                    %mo )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  V_{qu}(r)
                  \\;=\;
                  V_{cl}(r)
                  \\left(
                  1
                  \- \frac{135 + 2 N_\nu}{ 30 \pi^2}
                  \\frac{G \hbar}{r^2 c^2}
                  +
                  \\text{higher order}
                  \\right)
          %p
            (where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mi ν
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_\nu
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+number"} number
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass"} massless
            = succeed "-species," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neutrino"} neutrino
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravitational+constant"} gravitational constant
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/speed+of+light"} speed of light
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℏ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
            is
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck%27s+constant"} Planck's constant
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Donoghue95"} Donoghue 95, (65)
          %p
            Here for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r = 1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fm"} fm
            we have that the quantum correction is by a tiny factor of order
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                      %mi ℏ
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi r
                        %mn 2
                      %msup
                        %mi c
                        %mn 2
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mrow
                      %mi r
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                      %mi fm
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ∼
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mn 10
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 38
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{G \hbar}{r^2 c^2}\vert_{r = 1 fm}
                  \\;\sim\;
                  10^{-38}
                  \\,.
          %h3#vacuum_energy_and_cosmological_constant Vacuum energy and Cosmological constant
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalization
            freedom in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+QFT"} perturbative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of gravity induces freedom in the choice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum+expectation+value"} vacuum expectation value
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stress-energy+tensor"} stress-energy tensor
            and hence in the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
          %p
            For details see
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "cosmological+constant#InPerturbativeQuantumGravity"} there
          %h3#CorrectionToAnomalousMagneticMomentOfTheLeptons Anomalous magnetic moment of the leptons
          %p
            The corrections of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+order"} 1-loop
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+QFT"} perturbative
            quantum gravity to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anomalous+magnetic+moment"} anomalous magnetic moment
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electron"} electron
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/muon"} muon
            have been computed in (
            %a{:href => "#BerendsGastman75"}> Berends-Gastman 75
            ) and found to be finite, without need for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagrams"} Feynman diagrams
            contribute:
          %p
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/GravityVertexCorrectionsForQED.png", :width => "750"}/
          %h2#Approaches Approaches
          %p Some proposed proposals towards a more fundamental theory of quantum gravity:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+sets"} causal sets
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+dynamical+triangulation"} causal dynamical triangulation
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+principle"}> holographic principle
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+entanglement+entropy"} holographic entanglement entropy
                (
                %em>
                  %a{:href => "entanglement#ReferencesItFromQBit"} It from QBit
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/asymptotic+freedom"} asymptotic freedom
          %h2#special_cases Special cases
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+quantum+gravity"} 2d quantum gravity
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3d+quantum+gravity"} 3d quantum gravity
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/soft+graviton+theorem"}> soft graviton theorem
                \:
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/asymptotic+safety"} asymptotic safety
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+gravity+conjecture"} weak gravity conjecture
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Witten+conjecture"} Witten conjecture
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/piecewise+flat+spacetime"} piecewise flat spacetime
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ho%C5%99ava%E2%80%93Lifshitz+gravity"} Hořava–Lifshitz gravity
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general General
          %p Early mentioning of potential breakdown of classical geometry at microscopic scales:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bernhard+Riemann"}> Bernhard Riemann
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C3%9Cber+die+Hypothesen%2C+welche+der+Geometrie+zu+Grunde+liegen"} Über die Hypothesen, welche der Geometrie zu Grunde liegen
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-35121-1"} doi:10.1007/978-3-642-35121-1
              %p
                Engl. transl:
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Clifford"} William Clifford
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/On+the+hypotheses+which+underlie+geometry"} On the hypotheses which underlie geometry
                %strong VIII
                (1873) 183-184 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-26042-6"}> doi:10.1007/978-3-319-26042-6
                \&amp;rbrack;
              %blockquote
                %p
                  \&amp;lbrack;
                  %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/Riemann-OnTheHypotheses.pdf#page=11"}> §III.3
                  \&amp;rbrack;
                  %em Now it seems that the empirical notions on which the metric determinations of Space are based, the concept of a solid body and a light ray, lose their validity in the infinitely small; it is therefore quite definitely conceivable that the metric relations of Space in the infinitely small do not conform to the hypotheses of geometry; and in fact, one ought to assume this as soon as it permits a simpler way of explaining phenomena.
          %p
            Original discussion of the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wheeler-DeWitt+equation"} Wheeler-DeWitt equation
          %ul
            %li#DeWitt67
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bryce+DeWitt"}> Bryce DeWitt
              , (5.5) in:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Quantum Theory of Gravity. I. The Canonical Theory
              %strong 160
              (1967) 1113 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRev.160.1113"}> doi:10.1103/PhysRev.160.1113
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p Compilation of early articles:
          %ul
            %li
              Alexander S. Blum,
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dean+Rickles"} Dean Rickles
              (eds.):
              = succeed "," do
